-\paragraph{Feature and head-path ablations.} Supplementary Tables~\ref{supptab:feature_ablation} and~\ref{supptab:head_path} report the ablations referred to in the main text. All used the nine molecular and materials pools and the protocol of Fig.~\ref{fig:final_regret}. They ran for 40 seeds at $B = 30$, with the same initial designs, fidelity schedule and greedy policy. Only the HF head's inputs changed. Each TL surrogate was evaluated with its reported head on $[\,g^{(L)}, \varphi^{(L)}, 1\,]$. We then replaced $\varphi^{(L)}$ with second-hidden-layer features from an untrained network of the same architecture, retaining the scalar LF prediction $g^{(L)}$. A third configuration reduced the head to $[\,g^{(L)}, 1\,]$. The HF-only network uses the same architecture, training length and head, but fits HF observations alone. It starts from the same two HF points and spends the whole budget on HF evaluations. Because it has no LF network, it is listed once. The head-path ablation used TL-base, whose LF prediction enters both as the additive offset of $\mu^{(H)}$ and as a regression column. Each entry point was removed in turn, with learned and random features.
